description:
admin-shim/lib/letta-sdk-adapter.ts ~491-501:

  async close(): Promise<void> {
    this.dead = true;
    this.session?.close();   // session torn down immediately
    this.session = null;
  }

close() is called by housekeep() (idle eviction) and stopAll() (shutdown) without awaiting this.chain. A turn that passed the `if (this.dead)` guard before close() ran continues executing with this.session now null.

1. session.abort() is never called before close(), so the SDK-side pump may still be mid-frame when the session is closed.
2. The turn's finally block clears currentRunHandle/currentOnFrame while close() may be racing through the same state cleanup.
3. finalizeTurnLifecycle runs after the session is gone — safe today because it only touches disk, but fragile.

Fix: at the top of close(), await this.chain.catch(() => {}) before tearing down the session; also call session.abort() before session.close() so the SDK pump gets a clean shutdown signal.

admin-shim/lib/agent-pool.ts ~532-565: the spawning Map coalesce guard is set ONE microtask after the async IIFE fires:

  const p = (async () => {
    // ... await createAdapter + await w.start() ...
  })();                         // IIFE fires here

  this.spawning.set(key, p);   // guard set HERE, too late

Two concurrent get() calls (e.g. two simultaneous HTTP requests for the same cold agent) both evaluate this.spawning.get(key) before either sets it. Both create their own p, both await w.start(), both call this.workers.set(key, w). The second write wins; the first adapter is spawned, started, and silently orphaned — its subprocess keeps running but nothing holds a reference to close it.

Fix: move this.spawning.set(key, p) to BEFORE the (async () => {})() call so the guard is set synchronously before the first internal await yields control.
